---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/for/fashion';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'for/fashion');
const desc = description(site, 'for/fashion');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Should size be stored as a permanent customer fact?",
    "answer": "Treat it as an editable preference with product context. Sizes differ across silhouettes and brands, and a purchase may be a gift."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should one-time drops differ from restocks?",
    "answer": "A one-time release needs clear sold-out communication and alternatives. Core items can use exact-variant restock interest; do not promise a return of stock that is not planned."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should a back-in-stock demonstration show?",
    "answer": "Restock one size while another stays unavailable, then check eligibility, queue behavior and the linked variant. Document scarce-stock and sold-out handling."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should exchanges affect email?",
    "answer": "Use the actual return and exchange state to avoid conflicting offers and provide relevant fit help. A refund flag alone may not describe an ongoing exchange."
  },
  {
    "question": "When does a more complex platform pay off?",
    "answer": "When the required workflows and maintenance savings justify its total cost for your store. No universal monthly order threshold establishes that decision."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/for">← All store types</a>
    <p class="catalog-kicker mt-6">Fashion stores</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Choose Shopify email tools for sizes, drops and returns</h1>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify">
      <h2>Make inventory and fit part of selection</h2>
      <p>Apparel email needs to connect collection interest, the available size or color, and what happened after delivery. A store launching limited runs has a different job from a boutique replenishing core sizes. Select a tool using those workflows rather than a universal order-count threshold. The examples here are proposed operating cases, not reported brand experiments.</p>
      <p>Start with product, variant, collection, inventory availability, order and return state. A remembered size is a preference, not a guarantee across brands or silhouettes. Ask shoppers to confirm it when relevant and retain an unknown-preference path rather than silently excluding them from every launch.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify">
      <h2>Drop calendars and restocks are separate promises</h2>
      <p>A proposed drop plan confirms waitlist interest, explains the release time and timezone, and sends early access only to the promised audience. Check stock before the public release. Stop queued urgency when the item sells out and make the sold-out destination useful. Do not promise a restock for a one-time vintage piece or a non-repeating capsule.</p>
      <p>For replenished core products, subscribe interest to the exact variant. Klaviyo documents back-in-stock forms and flows for supported integrations, including Shopify; its form responds to the selected unavailable variant and targeting rules. That is scoped support for assessing it, not proof that your theme, inventory locations or scarce-stock release policy are configured correctly.</p>
      <p>Demonstrate a Medium restock while Large remains unavailable. Check a customer subscribed to two sizes, an item sold during the send queue and an inventory correction that is not real new supply. Agree who can pause the flow. A useful availability alert must still lead to a purchasable variant.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify">
      <h2>Fit education, exchanges and seasonal winback</h2>
      <p>After purchase, send approved measurements, fit notes, care guidance and a clear route to the actual return policy. Time follow-up using delivery information where available instead of a universal domestic try-on delay. Do not imply email has reduced returns without evidence.</p>
      <p>Return and exchange handling needs more than a refunded-order tag. A customer exchanging sizes may still want the product; someone with an unresolved fit issue may need help before a repeat offer. Shopify supports return and exchange notifications. Inspect customized templates and the source of return-state updates before using them to suppress marketing.</p>
      <p>Plan seasonal reactivation around available relevant collections and the merchandising calendar. Separate prior full-price and sale purchases for analysis without assuming every sale buyer refuses full price. Test useful styling or fit content against an offer where feasible, and calculate contribution after discounts and returns. There is no universal number of ignored messages that justifies a deep discount.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify">
      <h2>Build a shortlist around the demonstrated job</h2>
      <p>Include Klaviyo when variant restocks are central, using its documented back-in-stock support as the starting point for a store-specific demonstration. Compare Omnisend or Drip on the same size-selection, sold-out and return-state cases rather than assuming one is only for small stores. Ask how much custom logic the required behavior needs and who will maintain it through collection changes.</p>
      <p>If considering Sequenzy for assisted sequence creation, ask it to produce a reviewable drop journey with explicit waitlist eligibility, purchase exclusions and inventory checks. Confirm the actual Shopify connector and available events. A generated sequence does not establish predictive depth, variant-aware suppression or a lower total bill.</p>
      <p>When considering Privy for capture or email, evaluate the current products and plan scope directly. Avoid the blanket assertion that it can only collect addresses. Decide whether one system or separate capture and lifecycle owners can maintain the required consent, collection interest and suppression data without duplicate welcome messages.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify">
      <h2>Proposed acceptance cases and reporting</h2>
      <p>Use staff-controlled records for a new waitlist subscriber, a returning customer and an exchange customer. Exercise early access, public release, a purchase before the queued reminder, a sold-out size and an exchange to another size. Check both message eligibility and the destination product page. No real launch performance is claimed here.</p>
      <p>If adding SMS, establish channel eligibility separately and coordinate it with email and purchase status. Do not infer SMS permission from an email waitlist or prescribe a universal number of messages per drop. Document which owner stops both channels when stock is exhausted.</p>
      <p>Compare supplier quotes using actual contacts, launch frequency, restock sends, optional channels, connector work and operator time. Track full-price sell-through, net revenue after returns, second purchases by collection and opt-outs. A campaign click or attributed sale alone does not prove incremental profit; separate measured outcomes from assumptions in the review.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Documentation and next steps</h2><p>Primary documentation checked 24 November 2026. Documentation establishes only the stated product behavior; account configuration and the proposed workflows still need verification.</p><ul><li><a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/38767539287323">Klaviyo back-in-stock forms</a></li><li><a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/fulfillment/managing-orders/returns">Shopify returns and exchanges</a></li><li><a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/fulfillment/setup/notifications">Shopify store notifications</a></li></ul><p><a class="link" href="/apps">Explore the tool catalog</a> · <a class="link" href="/for">Compare store requirements</a></p></div></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Fashion stores email FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
